The [[security-oversight|Security Oversight Committee]] (twelve members of the Citizens' Chamber chosen by lot, four Assembly members from outside the Executive and the largest grouping, and a judge-Inspector) uses its full access. It finds:

- warrants were obtained for 3 of the 11 people surveilled, on evidence the judge was not told was thin;
- **8 were watched with no warrant at all**;
- a request to watch the party platform came from a party official, through a member of the Executive Council.

The Committee suspends the operation (Art. 85.3). Every person surveilled is entitled to see their file (Art. 16).
